      "parts": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\"When running inference with a pre‑trained model, the library needs to determine the batch size from the decoder inputs in order to allocate key/value cache tensors. The existing implementation assumes that the decoder input object always contains a `dims` array and directly accesses its first element. In cases where the decoder input is missing the `dims` property—such as when only an attention mask is supplied or when the input tensor is generated dynamically—this assumption leads to a runtime error because the code attempts to read an index of `undefined`. The failure interrupts model execution and prevents downstream tasks like text generation or speech synthesis from completing.\n\nThe required behavior is that the batch size calculation should safely handle the absence of a `dims` array. The logic must first try to obtain the dimensions from the primary decoder input; if that is unavailable, it should fall back to an alternative input (e.g., the attention mask). If neither provides a `dims` array, the code should default to a batch size of one rather than throwing. This ensures that the model can still run with minimal input information, using a sensible default for cache allocation.\n\nImplement a defensive check that uses optional property access when retrieving the `dims` array, and only extracts the first dimension when it exists. The fallback default of one should be applied whenever the dimensions cannot be determined. The change must be transparent to callers: the public API and model output remain unchanged, but the internal batch size computation becomes resilient to missing shape metadata.\"\n",
          "id": "prt_eed99d588001jhD9DMG2sK7XTj",
          "sessionID": "ses_112662a90ffe1DjuAxELVYt6cR",
